{:edb/db-before-t 2802 :edb/db-after-t 2803 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536115 50 #inst "2026-10-09T15:23:55.910Z" 13194139536115 true] [17592186124414 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384313 13194139536115 true] [17592186124498 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384306 13194139536115 true] [17592186124498 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384320 13194139536115 true] [17592186124909 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536115 true] [17592186124909 1100 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139536115 true] [17592186124909 1100 "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}, [x]_{S} = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}$." 13194139536115 false] [17592186124909 1145 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-8)-9\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536115 true] [17592186124950 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536115 true] [17592186124950 1100 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ to $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x =-3c_{1} - 2c_{2}$.\n\n$P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139536115 false] [17592186124950 1100 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536115 true] [17592186124950 1145 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=-3{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536115 true] [17592186384306 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384307 13194139536115 true] [17592186384306 1089 "q-82614" 13194139536115 true] [17592186384306 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536115 true] [17592186384306 1100 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139536115 true] [17592186384306 1113 #uuid "fdb130b3-f386-5a0d-a67b-84c93acff686" 13194139536115 true] [17592186384306 1145 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536115 true] [17592186